      "The other constraint is that your business will change. A name that describes exactly what you do in month one can become a liability in month eighteen, when the thing you actually sell is something else. Names with a little semantic distance from the product age better.",
    ],
    conventions: {
      heading: "What makes a startup name hold up",
      paragraphs: [
        "The dominant modern pattern is a short coined word — two syllables, four to eight letters, no fixed meaning. Coined names are almost always available, are far easier to trademark than dictionary words, and give you a clean slate to define. The cost is that they carry no information: you have to spend marketing budget teaching people what the word means, which is fine if you were going to spend that budget on category education anyway.",
        "The second pattern is the concrete real word used metaphorically — a physical noun that implies a quality rather than describing a feature. These are memorable and self-explanatory in tone if not in function, but the good ones are heavily contested on .com, which is why so many land on .io, .co, or a compound.",
        "The pattern to be careful with is the descriptive phrase. It buys immediate clarity and a small search-intent advantage, but it boxes you in, tends to be long, and often forces hyphens or awkward spellings once the obvious versions are gone. If you are pre-product-market-fit and expect to pivot, weight against it.",
        "Practical test before you commit: say the name down a phone line and have someone type what they heard. If they get it right without you spelling it, the name passes. If you find yourself saying \"with a K\" or \"no E at the end\", you will be saying that for years.",
      ],
    },
    patterns: [
      {
        pattern: "Coined two-syllable word",
        examples: "verolo, cadenza, oryn, lumira",
        why: "Almost always available, easy to trademark, no baggage. Needs marketing spend to give it meaning.",
      },
      {
        pattern: "Concrete noun, used metaphorically",
        examples: "keystone, thistle, harbor, ledger",
        why: "Memorable and easy to say. Signals a quality without describing a feature, so it survives a pivot.",
      },
      {
        pattern: "Two real words fused",
